  "sourceNotes": {
    "raw": "The compound verb '信得過' (trust/reliable) uses the potential complement '得過' to indicate capability or worthiness. This pattern [verb + 得過] appears frequently in Cantonese to express that something meets a standard. Note that '唔信得過' means 'cannot trust' or 'untrustworthy.'\n\nThe word '尋日' (yesterday) is distinctly Cantonese; Mandarin speakers say '昨天.' When asking 'how strange?' Cantonese speakers use '點奇怪法?' where '法' shows the manner or way something is done. Avoid saying '怎麼奇怪' as this sounds like Mandarin interference.\n\n'人工' specifically means 'salary' or 'wages' in Cantonese, not general 'human labor.' The particle '呀' at sentence end marks questions with a softer, more conversational tone than '嗎' in Mandarin. When describing job opportunities, '工作機會' is formal; casual speech might just say '份工' (a job)."
  }
